diagnosis_text:
I checked the core segment between R6 and R7 and found no current fault. OSPF is healthy and converged across the middle: on R6, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows Full with neighbors on eth0 to 46.1.1.1 (R4), on eth1 to 56.1.1.1 (R5), and on eth2 to 67.1.1.2 (R7). On R7, the OSPF adjacency to R6 on eth0 (67.1.1.2 ↔ 67.1.1.1) is Full/Backup. Both devices have rich OSPF-learned routing tables, and the interconnect is pingable (R6→R7 0% loss, sub‑ms RTT).

End-to-end traffic “across the middle” is fine: pc0 ↔ pc12 reachability is 0% loss in both directions, and direct probes from R6 and R7 to both pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) succeed without loss. Interfaces on R6 (eth0/1/2/3) and R7 (eth0/1) are UP with no RX/TX errors reported. If a core failure had occurred, we would expect OSPF adjacencies to drop and/or east–west pings to fail; neither is observed.

Localization: the key core link is R6 eth2 (67.1.1.1) to R7 eth0 (67.1.1.2); both are operational, and R6’s other core adjacencies (to 46.1.1.1 and 56.1.1.1) are also stable.

Root cause: No persistent fault on R6 or R7 at time of investigation; the alert was likely transient or a monitoring false positive.

Corrective fix: No action required on R6/R7. Recommend reviewing monitoring thresholds and correlating with OSPF/syslog events to avoid transient false alarms; keep alert active only on sustained adjacency loss. === END ===